      "output": {
        "question": "What academic mathematics book does Lisbeth Salander carry around and memorize?",
        "answer": "Lisbeth Salander carries around and memorizes the book *Dimensions in Mathematics*. This is explicitly stated in the passage [E1], where it mentions that she \"took out Dimensions in Mathematics\" from her bag. Additionally, the passage [E2] describes the book as a 1,200-page brick about the history of mathematics and notes that she was enticed into an enchanted world of figures when she opened it.",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_006.html",
            "paragraph": 87,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "8",
            "text": "She folded the paper, took a swig from the bottle of Carib, and then she saw the man from room 32 come out on the veranda from the bar. He had his brown briefcase in one hand and a glass of Coca-Cola in the other. His eyes swept over her without recognition before he sat on a bench at the other end of the veranda and fixed his gaze on the water beyond.\nHe seemed utterly preoccupied and sat there motionless for seven minutes, Salander observed, before he raised his glass and took three deep swallows. Then he put down the glass and resumed staring out to sea. After a while she opened her bag and took out Dimensions in Mathematics .",
            "paragraph_end": 88,
            "part_end": 1,
